+Reading from a file
+~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~
+
+The :func:`~rich.progress.open` function reads a file with a progress display, so a long running consumer (such as a JSON or CSV parser) can show how far through its input it is. Pass it a path and it will show progress as bytes are read. By default the file is opened in text mode (``'r'``) and a ``str`` is returned; use ``'rb'`` to receive ``bytes`` instead. The default ``total`` is the size of the file in bytes. If you already have a :class:`~rich.progress.Progress` instance, :meth:`~rich.progress.Progress.open` returns a reader that advances an existing task (or a new one) without starting the display. Here's an example::
+
+    import json
+    from rich.progress import open
+
+    with open("data.json") as file:
+        data = json.load(file)
+
+Reading from a file-like object
+~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~
+
+If you already have an open binary file, use :func:`~rich.progress.wrap_file` (or :meth:`~rich.progress.Progress.wrap_file` to update an existing task). Only binary files are supported. A ``total`` (the size of the file in bytes) is required, unless you pass a ``task_id`` for a task that already has a total. The wrapped file is not closed when the progress context exits. Here's an example::
+
+    import csv
+    import io
+    from rich.progress import wrap_file
+
+    data = io.BytesIO(b"a,b\n1,2\n")
+    with wrap_file(data, total=8) as reader:
+        rows = list(csv.reader(io.TextIOWrapper(reader, encoding="utf-8")))
+
